---
import { Code } from "astro:components";
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { url } from "../../lib/url";

const disk = `schema: 1
id: disk_full
role: issue
description: >-
  The runner ran out of disk - No space left on device / ENOSPC. The step
  that hit it is a victim of the runner, not the cause.
tags: [infra, disk]
match:
  any:
    - template: 'No space left on device'
    - template: 'ENOSPC'
window: { before: 3, after: 3 }`;

const ruff = `schema: 1
id: ruff
description: Ruff, the Python linter and formatter - lint violations and files ruff format would change.
tags: [python, lint, format]
define:
  FILE: '[\\w./-]+\\.pyi?'
  NUM: '\\d+'
  RULE: '[A-Z]+[0-9]+'
outputs:
  check:
    description: ruff check lint violations, each naming a rule code and the file:line it fired on.
    variants:
      concise:                       # older default: one line per violation
        match: { template: '$FILE:$NUM:$NUM: $RULE ' }
      full:                          # newer default: the code, then the location
        match:
          all:
            - regex: '^%{RULE} '
            - followed_by: { template: '--> $FILE:$NUM:$NUM', within: 2 }
        window: { after: 8 }
  format:
    role: gate
    description: ruff format --check listing the files it would reformat. Running ruff format fixes it.
    match: { regex: '^Would reformat: ' }`;

const config = `matchers:
  registries:                       # official is already declared; add your own
    acme: ./ci/matchers             # relative to this file
  include:
    - official/python               # a bundle: every matcher under matchers/python/
    - official/ruff                 # a matcher, with all its outputs
    - acme/mytool
  exclude:
    - official/ruff.format          # drop one output`;

const pinned = `matchers:
  registries:
    acme: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/acme/matchers/v1.4.0/   # a tag or a commit SHA`;

const tryIt = `ci-doctor matchers try matchers/python/ruff/matcher.yaml ./job.github.log
ci-doctor matchers try official/ruff ./job.log --framing gitlab`;
---

<Base
    title="Matchers"
    description="Matchers, outputs and variants, the five roles that rank their windows, bundles, and the registries a run loads them from."
>
    <h1>Matchers</h1>
    <p class="lead">
        A matcher recognises one tool's failure output and keeps the lines around
        it. ci-doctor ships none: every run loads the ones your config selects from
        the registries it declares.
    </p>

    <h2>Matcher, output, variant</h2>
    <p>
        A matcher file describes one tool, wrapper or environment issue. Inside it,
        each level has one job:
    </p>
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Is</th><th>The model sees</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td>Matcher</td><td>One tool, wrapper or issue</td><td>nothing of its own when it has several outputs</td><td><code>ruff</code>, <code>disk_full</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Output</td><td>One distinct output: one meaning, one description</td><td><code>ruff.check [tool]: …</code></td><td><code>ruff.check</code>, <code>ruff.format</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Variant</td><td>One spelling of that output, such as an older version's format</td><td>nothing: it shares the output's description</td><td><code>ruff.check (full)</code></td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
        A different meaning is a new output; the same meaning spelled differently is
        a variant. Every variant runs on every log, so whichever spelling the log
        holds picks itself: there is no tool version to declare. Most matchers have
        one output and write it at the top level:
    </p>
    <Code code={disk} lang="yaml" theme="github-dark" />
    <p>A tool with several outputs lists them, and gives a spelling its own window only when it needs one:</p>
    <Code code={ruff} lang="yaml" theme="github-dark" />

    <h2>Finding and keeping</h2>
    <p>
        <code>match</code> finds the anchor lines. A leaf is a <code>regex</code>
        (RE2), or a <code>template</code> written as the line looks in the log:
        everything is literal except <code>$NAME</code>, which inserts the matcher's
        own <code>define</code>, and <code>...</code>, which matches anything.
        <code>all</code>, <code>any</code> and <code>not</code> combine leaves on
        one line; <code>inside</code>, <code>preceded_by</code> and
        <code>followed_by</code> relate a line to the lines around it.
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>window</code> says which lines become evidence: <code>before</code>
        and <code>after</code>, or <code>from</code> a header above, <code>until</code>
        a closing line, <code>while</code> the next line continues, each capped by
        <code>max_lines</code>. Every field is on
        <a href={url("/reference/matcher-format/")}>Matcher format v1</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
        Everything compiles to the one RE2 pass per line ci-doctor has always run,
        plus a linear pass over the hits: a hundred matchers cost one scan of the
        log, and no pattern can hang a job.
    </p>

    <h2>Roles</h2>
    <p>
        <code>role</code> decides which windows survive when the evidence is over
        budget, and tells the model how to read them. Higher ranks survive:
    </p>
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Role</th><th>Means</th><th>Rank</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>issue</code></td><td>The environment failed: a full disk, out of memory, the network. Matched only on standard strings.</td><td>4</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>tool</code></td><td>The failing tool printing the cause: <code>tsc</code>, pytest, a traceback. The default.</td><td>3</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>gate</code></td><td>A deliberate check failing the job on purpose: coverage, a scanner, a formatter.</td><td>2</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>wrapper</code></td><td>An orchestrator reporting that something it ran failed: npm, make, gradle.</td><td>1</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>fallback</code></td><td>The generic catch-all.</td><td>0</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
        An <code>issue</code> outranks the tool it broke: shed the full disk and the
        model blames the compile error it caused; shed the compile error and the
        cause is still in front of it. A <code>gate</code> ranks below the tool
        because a failing test fails coverage too. A <code>wrapper</code> prints
        <em>after</em> the tool it ran, so it must lose to it wherever it sits.
        Within a role, the later window wins. The section's last lines are always
        kept as well, ranked below every matcher.
    </p>

    <h2>Bundles</h2>
    <p>
        A bundle is a named list of matchers and other bundles from the same
        registry. A registry generates one per ecosystem folder:
        <code>official/python</code> is every matcher under
        <code>matchers/python/</code>. Curated bundles, such as <code>all</code>,
        are written by hand. Bundles nest at most five deep, a cycle is an error,
        and a matcher reached twice loads once. <code>ci-doctor matchers list</code>
        shows which entry brought each matcher in.
    </p>

    <h2>Registries and refs</h2>
    <p>
        A registry is a folder with an <code>index.yaml</code> listing every
        matcher and bundle it serves. Your config names registries under
        <code>matchers.registries</code>, alias → location, and selects from them
        under <code>matchers.include</code>, each entry an <code>alias/name</code>
        ref to a matcher or a bundle. Including a matcher includes all its outputs;
        <code>matchers.exclude</code> drops one by its dotted id. One registry is
        declared for you: <code>official</code>, at
        <code>https://matchers.cidoc.dev/</code>. Nothing is included for you.
    </p>
    <Code code={config} lang="yaml" theme="github-dark" />
    <p>
        Before analysis starts, each run reads every registry an include names,
        expands the bundles, and fetches, validates and compiles each selected
        matcher. Every read is bounded: an index of at most 1 MiB and 2,000
        entries, a file of at most 64 KiB, paths that cannot leave the registry,
        and a redirect that cannot drop from <code>https</code> to
        <code>http</code>. Anything wrong, including an empty <code>include</code>,
        an unknown alias or name, an <code>exclude</code> that names no output, or
        an unreachable registry, is a broken setup: the job exits 1, naming the
        registry and the cause. <code>ci-doctor config --validate</code> runs the
        same steps as a dry run.
    </p>

    <h2>Schema versions</h2>
    <p>
        Every matcher file says which format it is written in
        (<code>schema: 1</code>). Every ci-doctor compiles every schema version it
        knows, and a released version never changes how it compiles. A matcher in a
        newer version than your ci-doctor knows is skipped with a warning when a
        bundle brings it in, and fails the setup when you included it by name.
    </p>

    <h2>Pinning, and running with no network</h2>
    <p>
        There is no cache and no lock file: every run reads each registry as it is
        now. That keeps a fix to a matcher one push away from every pipeline, and it
        means a registry change reaches your next run unreviewed. To hold a
        registry still, point at a URL that contains a fixed revision.
    </p>
    <Code code={pinned} lang="yaml" theme="github-dark" />
    <p>
        Registries are the one network dependency besides your CI provider and your
        LLM. On an air-gapped network, clone the registry inside it and point the
        alias at the local path; see <a href={url("/reference/registry/")}>Hosting a registry</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Finding, trying and writing matchers</h2>
    <p>
        The official catalogue, with each matcher's fixtures beside what it selects
        from them, is at <a href="https://matchers.cidoc.dev">matchers.cidoc.dev</a>.
        From the terminal, <code>ci-doctor matchers search</code> and
        <code>ci-doctor matchers info</code> read the same indexes. To see what a
        matcher selects from your own log, with the real engine:
    </p>
    <Code code={tryIt} lang="sh" theme="github-dark" />
    <p>
        It marks each anchor and names the variant that fired. Writing and testing
        matchers in a registry of your own is on
        <a href={url("/reference/registry/")}>Hosting a registry</a>; contributing
        to the official one is in the
        <a href="https://github.com/ci-doctor/matchers">ci-doctor/matchers</a>
        repository.
    </p>
</Base>
